rtsp: what was sent before a PLAY is not read as part of what it sends

A PLAY that follows another one in a session, after a seek or a pause,
is answered while packets the source sent before it was told are still
on their way. Inside the connection they are thrown away with whatever
else arrives before the answer. As datagrams they arrive after it, and
are read as the first packets of the new place: timed by the clock the
answer set, which puts them before the place that was asked for, by as
much as the source's RTP clock moved between the two. A seek forward in
a recording then begins with a keyframe and some sound of the place that
was left, and a gap behind them.

The answer says which packet is the first it sends (RTP-Info, seq), so
that a client can tell the two apart. That was parsed and not used. A
packet from before that sequence number is now dropped, for a second
after the answer, which is longer than a datagram is under way. It is
looked for only after a PLAY that follows another: before the first of
a session nothing was sent, and a source that states a wrong number
there costs nothing. One that states a wrong number later costs that
second and no more.

A sender report can be on its way in the same manner, and does more
harm when it is: the first report read after a PLAY is what the times
of everything the PLAY sends are counted from. A source may set its RTP
clock anew with a PLAY, which is what the answer's rtptime is there to
say, and a report made before it then puts the whole of the new place
at the wrong time, by as much as the clock was moved. A report has no
sequence number. It is told by its RTP time, which for one made before
the PLAY is before the rtptime the answer gave, and is passed over for
the same second. The times are then counted from the answer alone until
the next report comes, as they are while none has come yet.

